 "result": "**Mechanism:** a single `ContextVar` plus werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects that read from it.\n\n- `src/tessel/globals.py:40`: `_app_var = ContextVar(\"tessel.app_ctx\")` holds the current `AppContext`.\n- `src/tessel/globals.py:44-62`: each global is a `LocalProxy` bound to `_app_var`, with an attribute name to pull off the context:\n  - `active_app` → `ctx.app` (line 44)\n  - `g` → `ctx.g` (line 47)\n  - `request` → `ctx.request` (line 57)\n  - `session` → `ctx.session` (line 60)\n  - `app_ctx` → the context itself (line 41)\n- `src/tessel/ctx.py:433`: `AppContext.push()` does `_app_var.set(self)` and saves the token. `ctx.py:498` calls `_app_var.reset(token)` on pop. Because it's a ContextVar, each thread or coroutine sees its own context.\n- If nothing is pushed, the proxies raise a \"Working outside of application/request context\" error (`_no_app_msg` / `_no_req_msg`, `globals.py:36-56`).\n\n`request` and `session` also need the context to have a request. Internal code reads `_app_var.get(None)` directly, e.g. `ctx.py:232` and `helpers.py:127`.\n\nThe `vendor/python/tessel-3.1.x` copies are the same code.",
